<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Tahirid Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1454 CE – 1517 CE · capital Rada'a</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Sunni dynasty of medieval Yemen, successors of the Rasulids, swept away by the Mamluk and Ottoman invasions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Tahir were a family from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada'a</a> and Juban region who served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rasulid-dynasty">Rasulid dynasty</a>. As Rasulid rule collapsed in the 1440s, the brothers Ali and Amir ibn Tahir seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and by 1454 had made themselves sultans of lowland and southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada'a</a>, Juban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Ta'izz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and inherited the Rasulid tax system and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden's</a> trade, though with less wealth. They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> patrons of learning; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiriya-madrasa">Amiriya Madrasa</a> at Rada'a, completed in 1504, with exceptional painted interiors. They fought the Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> for the highlands and Sanaa.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1515 a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> fleet arrived to fight the Portuguese and turned on the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a>. Amir was killed in 1517, the year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Remnants held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> until 1538, when <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yemen-eyalet">Yemen Eyalet</a> began.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tahirid-sultanate.jpg" alt="Tahirid Sultanate" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: كنز المعلومات · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahiride_Dynasty_yemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1454 CE – 1517 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rada'a</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali ibn Tahir, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Dawud, Amir ibn Abd al-Wahhab</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tahirids of Yemen, Banu Tahir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=tahirid-sultanate" aria-label="Tahirid Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5907 1641 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tahirid Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6227 1897L6206 1928M6227 1897L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6206 1928h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6227 1897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6227 1897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rada'a. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=tahirid-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
